==German==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gmh|de}} {{term|anvahen||lang=gmh|to grab on}}, {{term|an||lang=gmh|on}} + {{term|vahen||lang=gmh|to grab}}, corresponding to {{prefix|an|fangen|lang=de}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈanˌfaŋən/|[ˈanˌfaŋən]|[ˈanˌfaŋŋ̩]|lang=de}}
* {{hyphenation|an|fan|gen|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-at-anfangen.ogg|audio (Austria)}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-strong|class=7|fängt an|fing an|angefangen|auxiliary=[[haben]] or [[sein]]}}

# {{context|intransitive|lang=de}} to [[begin]]; to [[commence]]
#: ''Das Konzert '''fängt''' gleich '''an'''.''
#:: The concert is '''beginning''' shortly.
# {{context|intransitive|with {{l/de|zu}} + infinitive|lang=de}} to [[start]] doing something
#: ''Wann hast du '''angefangen''' zu rauchen?''
#:: When did you '''start''' smoking?
# {{context|intransitive|with {{l/de|mit}}|lang=de}} to [[begin]] something; to [[start]] something
#: ''Morgen '''fangen''' wir mit dem Projekt '''an'''.''
#:: Tomorrow we are '''starting''' the project.
# {{context|transitive|colloquial|lang=de}} to [[begin]] something; to [[start]] something
#: ''Wann hast du das Rauchen '''angefangen'''?''
#:: When did you '''start''' smoking?
#: ''Morgen '''fangen''' wir das Projekt '''an'''.''
#:: Tomorrow we are '''starting''' the project.

====Usage notes====
* The normal auxiliary for this verb is {{term|haben|lang=de}}: ''Ich habe angefangen.'' – "I have begun." The auxiliary {{term|sein|lang=de}} is used by some speakers in western and northern Germany: ''Ich bin angefangen.'' This is, however, widely restricted to colloquial usage. (Cf. the same construction in Dutch {{term|beginnen||to begin|lang=nl}}: ''Ik ben begonnen.'')
* The construction of ''anfangen'' as a transitive verb (sense 4) is perfectly acceptable in spoken German, but would not be common in formal writing.

